RemoteBuild replies 'Plugin loaded' with an empty error even when the engine could not resolve the plugin's dependencies, so B097's fail-fast guard in run_batch never fires


## Measured by lane-resolver during B123, 2026-09-01

## The observation

`probe_enum_corpus.run_batch` guards with:

    if loaded.get("error"): raise SystemExit(...)

That guard was added by B097 so a failed load STOPS the run rather than looking
merely inconclusive. Measured today, for a plugin whose dependency cannot be
resolved, RemoteBuild replies:

    {"error": "", "data": "Plugin loaded"}

while the engine logs the dependency failure **and** `Loaded plugin` anyway. So
the guard never fires, no name is analysed, and the run produces exactly the
outcome the guard exists to prevent — through the one door it does not cover.

## The general shape, and why this is worth its own ticket

**"I delivered your request" and "your build succeeded" are different
propositions.** The transport is answering the first; the guard is reading it as
the second.

They were conflated for an honest reason: **before B097 the load reply was inert
and carried no information at all**, because `load_plugin` had never once
worked. It became load-bearing the day the transport started working, and **not
one line of the consumer changed.** A value that was decoration becoming
meaningful is a contract change that no diff records and no test fails on.

This is the same family as B118's "the engine refused this" vs "this build did
not happen", and it is the second time in one batch that a newly-working
transport silently promoted a dead value into a live one.

## Where the fix belongs — and where it does NOT

**Do not fix this by tightening the guard alone.** The reply is genuinely
truthful about what it reports: the plugin WAS loaded. The defect is that load
success is being used as a proxy for build success, and B118 already built the
thing that answers the real question — four-valued `build_status` sliced by the
build's own markers. Route the decision through that.

If a plugin-side change to RemoteBuild's reply looks attractive, note that
`~/src/tm-remote-build` and `~/src/openplanet/my-plugins/tm-remote-build` are
**Max's, with uncommitted work, and are not ours to edit.** Read only. Fix this
on our side.

## Test axes

The subject is a MISSING failure, so build the pairs and mutation-test each
guard — see the hazards catalogue, and note that B118 found 4 of 9 such guards
vacuous because an earlier check had already decided the case.

- Dependency unresolvable vs. dependency present. Must differ.
- Compile failure with `Loaded plugin` logged after it, vs. a real clean load.
  Must differ.
- Load genuinely refused (if any route still produces that) vs. load accepted.
- A run where the guard SHOULD fire and one where it should not — an over-condemn
  mutation that fails everything must be caught, exactly as
  `just enum-probe-mutants` now catches for B123.